<!--
  RM_WaxSuit.xml                     CRACKEDLANDS_RULED_CONTENT_1 (FOUNDRY build)

  design/Jawa/worldbuilding/biomes/cracked_lands_bedazzle_cast_2026-09-28.md
  §"The wax suit — crack-wax sealed underwater suit": "a full-body sealed
  apparel piece, crack-wax over a stiffened frame, that lets a pawn survive
  water and underwater TERRAIN ... Terrain survival ONLY ... no sea-floor
  access ... no pawn dive verb, mechanism or menu option anywhere in this
  feature ... the same reconciliation WARCASKET_SUIT_CLASS_1 carries: an
  apparel class may make hostile terrain survivable; it may never become a
  travel mechanism."

  🔴 SEA-FLOOR / SHIP-ONLY LAW UNTOUCHED: this def grants no dive verb, no
  MapPortal access, no sea-floor teleport of any kind. `RM_SeaDiveHatch`
  (DivingInteraction) remains the only way onto a sea floor. A future
  reader: if this suit looks like it should let a pawn dive, that reading
  is wrong — see CRACKEDLANDS_RULED_CONTENT_1 step 6 and
  CRACKEDLANDS_MECHANICS_BUILD_1's own "no pawn dive verb anywhere in this
  item" watch-out.

  TERRAIN-SURVIVAL MECHANISM — SEARCHED, NOT FOUND REUSABLE, NOT WIRED:
  This pass searched for an existing water/underwater-terrain-survival
  stat or hediff to reuse rather than invent one, per this build's own
  brief. Found:
    - `WARCASKET_SUIT_CLASS_1` is a NAMED RECONCILIATION LINE (this file's
      header above, and CRACKEDLANDS_RULED_CONTENT_1 step 6), not a built
      item and not a mechanism — grep across src/ and
      infrastructure/state/items/ turns up no such queue item and no
      StatDef/HediffDef by that name. There is nothing there to reuse.
    - RUT_SealedSleeper's crack-wax mechanism (`CompWaterWakeTrigger`) is a
      WAKE trigger for a dormant creature, not a pawn survival mechanism —
      irrelevant to a pawn standing in water.
    - `RM_PitDrowning` (src/RimMandrake/FlowWorks/Defs/Pits/HediffDefs/
      Pit_Hediffs.xml) is a real, shipped drowning hediff — but read its
      trigger, CompPitFitting.CanSwim() (FlowWorks/Source/Pits/Fitting/
      CompPitFitting.cs): immunity is checked ONLY via
      `ImmunityHandler.AnyGeneMakesFullyImmuneTo(RM_PitDrowning)` — a GENE
      check, with no apparel/stat hook at all (apparel cannot grant a gene).
      It is also scoped to `CompPitFitting`-bearing pit cells (the
      FlowWorks excavation-engine "Building_OpenPit" mechanic), not to
      general open biome terrain — the Cracked Lands' 27 measured
      open-water tiles (freeze ruling R6, `RM_FloodedCanyon_GameCondition
      Defs.xml`'s flood water) are ordinary map terrain, not Pit cells, so
      even a gene-immunity route would not reach them without new wiring.
    - No vanilla RaceProperties/ApparelProperties flag or StatDef for
      "survives deep/lethal water" was found either (same absence
      RM_PitDrowning's own header already documents from its 2026-09
      search).
  Conclusion: no existing stat/hediff reaches "a pawn wearing this survives
  the biome's lethal water terrain" without new C# or a new StatDef/
  HediffDef — both declined per this build's own instruction. This def
  therefore ships WITHOUT the survival mechanism wired. The suit is a
  complete, craftable, wearable apparel piece with real stats; equipping
  it today does not yet stop a pawn drowning in the Cracked Lands' open
  water. Owed to `CRACKEDLANDS_MECHANICS_BUILD_1` (that item's own scope
  leans on "existing machinery" for everything else it owns; a general
  open-terrain drowning hediff + an apparel-readable immunity/resistance
  hook is new work, not a rewire of RM_PitDrowning, since RM_PitDrowning's
  own gene-only design is deliberate per its header and not something to
  repurpose for apparel without discussion).

  SHAPE: closest sibling in this exact codebase is RUT_SealedSuit
  (UtinniPatches, "heaviest piece" of the Greentide kit tree — a full-body
  ParentName="ApparelMakeableBase" sealed suit crafted from a biome
  material + fabric/frame, Middle+Shell layers, heavy MoveSpeed penalty).
  Mirrored directly: same body-part-group/layer shape, same
  ElectricTailoringBench/HandTailoringBench recipe pattern. Worn-graphic
  art reuses vanilla's real Vacsuit paperdoll (same placeholder-with-real-
  shipped-art move RUT_SealedSuit and RM_ChillHeatedSuit both already make
  in this codebase) tinted crack-wax pale; the new bedazzle-commissioned
  icon (RM_CrackWaxSuit, already generated and copied into this mod's
  Textures/ this pass) is the inventory/ground icon.

  RM_ tier per Q11a (free mod looks the same as the campaign one); the
  Utinni-side "discovered here" research/recipe UNLOCK gating named in the
  bible ("crack-wax is this biome's material ... the recipe is discovered
  here") is NOT built this pass — this ships as an always-available
  recipe. Follow-up flagged, not owed to any existing item: a
  RUT_/Utinni-tier research-unlock wrapper around this same defName, if
  the owner wants the craft gated behind a Cracked-Lands-specific
  discovery the way the Swale is gated (CRACKEDLANDS_MECHANICS_BUILD_1
  §1's "LOCKED until discovered" shape).

  🔴 KNOWN GAP, FLAGGED RATHER THAN SILENTLY RESOLVED: this recipe's
  costList references `RUT_CrackWax` by the bible's own explicit
  instruction ("crafted from RUT_CrackWax") — but that ThingDef is
  currently defined ONLY in `mandrake.rut.patches` (UtinniPatches, the
  Utinni-campaign-only patch layer, About.xml: "LOAD LAST ... after every
  mod it touches"), which this mod's own About.xml does NOT depend on and
  should not, per its own description ("nothing here knows what campaign
  or planet uses it"). So this recipe is only actually craftable when
  UtinniPatches happens to be active alongside this mod — standalone
  RM_-only play cannot resolve the ingredient. This is the SAME shape of
  gap the cast bible itself flags for `RUT_SealedSleeper`/`RUT_EmperorVulture`
  ("today missing from the RM_ patch entirely") and is not something this
  file resolves — it is a roster/patch-layer wiring question
  (CRACKEDLANDS_RULED_CONTENT_1 step 2's own territory, owned this pass by
  a different FOUNDRY agent) rather than a new-apparel-def one. Not
  changed here: this mod's About.xml dependency list, or the roster/patch
  files. If the resolution is "give the RM_ tier its own RM_CrackWax
  duplicate resource" (the Greatbole precedent's shape), that is a content
  decision for whoever owns that gap, not assumed here.

  BAROQUE_CORPSE_MARKETVALUE_CRASH_1: guarded the costList entry below with
  MayRequire="mandrake.rut.patches" — same unguarded-cross-tier-reference
  shape as RM_MuttavaqUttaqar.xml's RUT_CrackWax butcherProducts entry
  (same item, same fix), which crashed RimWorld's implied-corpse-def
  generation and triggered the engine's silent corrupted-mods recovery.
  This costList entry is the identical List<ThingDefCountClass> mechanism
  and was one dereference away from the same class of NRE (StatWorker_
  MarketValue / CostListCalculator) the moment anything computes this
  suit's market value without UtinniPatches loaded. The guard only stops
  the crash — it does NOT resolve the gap above: the suit is still craftable
  everywhere (About.xml/roster untouched, per the note above), just for
  Steel+ComponentIndustrial alone whenever UtinniPatches is absent, and for
  the full bible-specified cost whenever it is present.
-->
<Defs>

  <ThingDef ParentName="ApparelMakeableBase">
    <defName>RM_CrackWaxSuit</defName>
    <label>crack-wax suit</label>
    <description>A full-body suit of stiffened fabric over a rigid frame, sealed watertight under a coat of smoothed crack-wax — the same waterproofing the Sealed shed from their burrows and the Farmers line their cisterns with, now worn. Heavy, slow and hot, but it keeps a pawn dry and breathing while they cross or work the Cracked Lands' flooded ground.\n\nTerrain only. This suit does not let anyone dive to a sea floor or come back from one — nothing does, except a ship.</description>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Things/Pawn/Humanlike/Apparel/RM_CrackWaxSuit/RM_CrackWaxSuit</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
      <drawSize>0.9</drawSize>
      <color>(222, 210, 176)</color>
    </graphicData>
    <techLevel>Industrial</techLevel>
    <tradeability>Sellable</tradeability>
    <costStuffCount>50</costStuffCount>
    <stuffCategories>
      <li>Fabric</li>
      <li>Leathery</li>
    </stuffCategories>
    <costList>
      <RUT_CrackWax MayRequire="mandrake.rut.patches">25</RUT_CrackWax>
      <Steel>6</Steel>
      <ComponentIndustrial>2</ComponentIndustrial>
    </costList>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>160</MaxHitPoints>
      <WorkToMake>24000</WorkToMake>
      <Mass>8</Mass>
      <Flammability>0.3</Flammability>
      <StuffEffectMultiplierArmor>0.15</StuffEffectMultiplierArmor>
      <StuffEffectMultiplierInsulation_Cold>0.2</StuffEffectMultiplierInsulation_Cold>
      <StuffEffectMultiplierInsulation_Heat>0.2</StuffEffectMultiplierInsulation_Heat>
      <ArmorRating_Sharp>0.15</ArmorRating_Sharp>
      <ArmorRating_Blunt>0.08</ArmorRating_Blunt>
      <EquipDelay>8</EquipDelay>
    </statBases>
    <equippedStatOffsets>
      <!-- Heavy move/work penalty appropriate to a sealed suit, per the
           bible. No terrain-survival stat offset here — see file header;
           nothing exists yet for this apparel to grant. -->
      <MoveSpeed>-0.6</MoveSpeed>
      <WorkSpeedGlobal>-0.15</WorkSpeedGlobal>
    </equippedStatOffsets>
    <thingCategories>
      <li>ApparelMisc</li>
    </thingCategories>
    <apparel>
      <bodyPartGroups>
        <li>Torso</li>
        <li>Neck</li>
        <li>Shoulders</li>
        <li>Arms</li>
        <li>Legs</li>
      </bodyPartGroups>
      <wornGraphicPath>Things/Pawn/Humanlike/Apparel/Vacsuit/Vacsuit</wornGraphicPath>
      <layers>
        <li>Middle</li>
        <li>Shell</li>
      </layers>
      <tags>
        <li>IndustrialAdvanced</li>
      </tags>
      <defaultOutfitTags>
        <li>Worker</li>
      </defaultOutfitTags>
    </apparel>
    <recipeMaker>
      <recipeUsers>
        <li>ElectricTailoringBench</li>
        <li>HandTailoringBench</li>
      </recipeUsers>
      <displayPriority>231</displayPriority>
    </recipeMaker>
    <tradeTags>
      <li>BasicClothing</li>
    </tradeTags>
  </ThingDef>

</Defs>
